DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffllooppeenn() function opens or creates a file and acquires an exclusive
     lock on it.  It is essentially equivalent with calling ooppeenn() with the
     same parameters followed by fflloocckk() with an _o_p_e_r_a_t_i_o_n argument of
     LOCK_EX, except that ffllooppeenn() will attempt to detect and handle races
     that may occur between opening / creating the file and locking it.  Thus,
     it is well suited for opening lock files, PID files, spool files, mail‐
     boxes and other kinds of files which are used for synchronization between
     processes.

     If _f_l_a_g_s includes O_NONBLOCK and the file is already locked, ffllooppeenn()
     will fail and set _e_r_r_n_o to EWOULDBLOCK.

     As with ooppeenn(), the additional _m_o_d_e argument is required if _f_l_a_g_s in‐
     cludes O_CREAT.

     The ffllooppeennaatt() function is equivalent to the ffllooppeenn() function except in
     the case where the _p_a_t_h specifies a relative path.  In this case the file
     to be opened is determined relative to the directory associated with the
     file descriptor _f_d instead of the current working directory.  If
     ffllooppeennaatt() is passed the special value AT_FDCWD in the _f_d parameter, the
     current working directory is used and the behavior is identical to a call
     to ffllooppeenn().

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If successful, ffllooppeenn() returns a valid file descriptor.  Otherwise, it
     returns -1, and sets _e_r_r_n_o as described in flock(2) and open(2).

